index 8c27f1d..c56acb5 100644
--- a/crates/rightkit-child-process/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/crates/rightkit-child-process/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -1,5 +1,30 @@
 # Changelog
 
+## 0.5.3
+
+Issues #733, #737, #455, #680, #402, #403, #391 (child-process half), #592, #684, #694, #685. Additive only: no existing public item changed shape, so dependents keep `rightkit-child-process = "0.5"`. Written without compiling or running Cargo here; CI builds and runs the tests.
+
+### New
+
+- **`AdoptedProcess::is_running` is zombie-aware, and `AdoptedProcess::try_reap`** (#733, GenRight). On Unix a process that exited but was not yet reaped (including an exited child of this process that nobody waited for) used to read as running, because `kill(pid, 0)` succeeds on a zombie; wait loops over a dead child spun to their timeout. `is_running` now reads the process state (Linux and Android: `/proc/<pid>/stat`; macOS: `proc_pidinfo` `pbi_status`) and returns `false` for a zombie. `try_reap() -> io::Result<Option<ExitStatus>>` collects the status of an exited **own child** with `waitpid(WNOHANG)` (`Ok(None)` when it is running, not our child, or already reaped; Windows has nothing to reap). Other Unix hosts cannot tell a zombie and keep the old answer. Do not `try_reap` a process an `OwnedChild` or `std::process::Child` still owns.
+- **`OwnedCommand::output(timeout, max_bytes)`, `output_with_stdin(input, timeout, max_bytes)`, `status(timeout)`, `status_with_timeout(timeout)`** (#455 HeardRight, #737 ScrapeRight). The blocking equivalents of `std::process::Command::output` and `status` with a deadline: the whole tree is terminated at the deadline, `output` captures stdout and stderr up to `max_bytes` each (the rest drained and discarded, `CapturedOutput::truncated` says so) and returns a `RunOutput { status, timed_out, stdout, stderr }`; `status` returns `WaitOutcome::Exited` or `WaitOutcome::Terminated`. The command's own window and containment settings are kept.
+- **`run_with_stdin(program, args, input, &RunOptions)`** (#680 HeardRight): `run` with bytes written to the child's stdin, which is then closed. `RunOptions` is unchanged (still `Copy`). The bytes are written on a short-lived thread (`rightkit-run-stdin`), so a child that never reads stdin cannot hold the call past its deadline, and a child that exits before reading everything is not an error.
+- **`StderrTail` can split on carriage returns** (#737, yt-dlp progress). New `LineSplit { Newline, NewlineOrCarriageReturn }` (`#[non_exhaustive]`, default `Newline`, so existing behaviour is unchanged) and `StderrTail::{plain_split, capture_split, try_capture_split}`. With `NewlineOrCarriageReturn` every `\r`-separated segment of a redrawn progress line is a line; `\r\n` is one break, empty segments are skipped, and the redaction, oversized-line marker and byte budget apply to each segment.
+- **`ProcessQuery::exe_prefix(dir)` and `ProcessQuery::arg_containing_ignore_case(needle)`** (#737). `exe_prefix` matches any process whose executable is at or below a directory (by whole path components; case-insensitive on Windows; the Unix directory is also tried canonicalized) and may be combined with `exe`. `arg_containing_ignore_case` is `arg_containing` with Unicode lower-casing of needle and argument. Both take part in the "at least one criterion" rule and refuse empty values.
+- **`AdoptedIdentity::request_quit()` and `AdoptedProcess::request_quit()`** (#402, Pulse): ask an application to quit through its own quit path, identity-checked like `signal`. Windows: `WM_CLOSE` posted to every top-level window of the process, after the process is proven to be the remembered one with the same handle (a process without a top-level window fails with `Unsupported`). macOS: a `kAEQuitApplication` Apple event addressed to the pid (`AECreateAppleEvent` / `AESendMessage` from CoreServices, `kAENoReply | kAENeverInteract`; `PermissionDenied` when Automation access is refused, `Gone` for `procNotFound`). Other Unix: `SIGTERM`. Delivery is reported, not the quit. `AdoptedSignal` is unchanged. New Windows feature `Win32_UI_WindowsAndMessaging`.
+- **`process_usage_of(pid)`, `process_usage_ext()`, `process_usage_ext_of(pid)`, `ProcessUsageExt { usage, thread_count }`** (#403 Pulse; #694 CodeRight's per-pid resident and commit sample). The same figures as `process_usage` for any pid (resident is `resident_bytes`, commit is `footprint_bytes`: Windows `PrivateUsage`): Linux from `/proc/<pid>` (`stat` ticks for CPU, `statm`, `io` bytes, `num_threads`), macOS from `proc_pid_rusage` (CPU converted from Mach ticks) and `proc_pidinfo` `pti_threadnum`, Windows from `GetProcessMemoryInfo`, `GetProcessTimes`, `GetProcessIoCounters` (`None` when the process cannot be opened that wide) and the process entry of `NtQuerySystemInformation` (thread count and context switches). A pid that names no process, or one that exited, is `NotFound`. `ProcessUsage` itself is unchanged; the thread count lives on `ProcessUsageExt`. On Linux the current process now also reports `disk_read_bytes` and `disk_write_bytes` from `/proc/self/io` when readable.
+- **`soak_sample(pid, &SoakOptions, &mut impl Write) -> io::Result<SoakReport>`** (#403): samples a pid at an interval and writes one NDJSON object per line (`t_ms`, `unix_ms`, `pid`, `resident_bytes`, `footprint_bytes`, `cpu_seconds`, `cpu_percent`, `thread_count`, `wakeups_total`, `disk_ops_total`, `disk_read_bytes`, `disk_write_bytes`; `null` for a figure the host cannot report), flushed per line. `SoakOptions { interval, duration, max_samples, stop }` bounds it; it also ends when the process exits (`SoakEnd::ProcessExited`). It blocks the calling thread and starts none.
+- **`process_tree_usage()`, `process_tree_usage_of(pid)`, `sum_process_tree(rows, root)`, `ProcessTreeUsage`** (#391, ViewRight `pdf_bench`): resident bytes of a process and every descendant (`root_rss_bytes`, `tree_rss_bytes`, `process_count`, `unreadable_count`), built on the 0.5.2 `process_table()` snapshot (one pass, no shell-out, nothing kept). The pure `sum_process_tree` walks any `ProcessEntry` table and is safe against parent loops and duplicate rows. The licence half of #391 belongs to `rightkit-license`.
+- **`AdoptedIdentity::terminate_tree(TerminationOptions) -> AdoptedTermination { confirmed_gone, sweep }`** (#592, CodeRight daemon takeover). Stops a process tree this program did not spawn: refuses this process, records the descendants while the root is proven, on Unix signals the root's process group (when it leads one) with `SIGTERM`, the grace, then `SIGKILL` while the identity still matches, then sweeps whatever recorded processes remain through the identity-checked `sweep`; Windows sweeps leaves first. `confirmed_gone` is true only when the root and every recorded descendant read as gone (and the group is empty). A pid that now names a different process is refused and reported.
+- **`AdoptedIdentity::children()` and `AdoptedIdentity::current()`** (#694): the direct children of a process with the same start-time guard as `descendants`; executable names are on each identity's `exe()`.
+- **`process_group_exists(pgid)`, `wait_process_group_gone(pgid, timeout)` (Unix), `tokio_support::process_group_exists` and `tokio_support::wait_group_gone` (feature `tokio`), `WindowsJob::active_process_count()` and `WindowsJob::is_empty()`** (#684, #556 addendum): public liveness probes to confirm a tree is gone after `terminate_tree`. Group ids 0 and 1 are never probed (libc maps `killpg(1, ..)` to `kill(-1, ..)`). `EPERM` counts as existing.
+- **`WindowsJobBuilder::breakaway_ok(bool)`, `WindowsJob::contain_current_process_allowing_breakaway()`, `WindowsJob::contain_current_process_with(builder)`** (#685): a kill-on-close job with `JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_BREAKAWAY_OK`, so one deliberately detached child can be spawned with `CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB` while every other descendant stays contained.
+
+### Already delivered (recorded here, not changed)
+
+- #540 (stall watchdog over a heartbeat file): `HeartbeatWatch` / `HeartbeatConfig` / `HeartbeatEvent` in `stall.rs`.
+- #402 `run`, `open_url`, `launch_app` (`run.rs`, `launch.rs`); #292 `OwnedCommand::spawn_suspended` / `OwnedChild::resume` / `is_suspended` (0.4.3); #682 `in_job_detached_flags(hidden, ..)` uses `CREATE_NO_WINDOW | CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP` in the in-job detached fallback (`process_windows.rs`, `spawn_windows.rs`).
+
 ## 0.5.2
 
 Issue bogusyogi/rightkit#480. The `process_table` tests pass on macOS arm64; Windows is verified on the Dell before publication.
